  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nTonight-Wed...High pressure to the north and lower pressure over\nthe Bahamas will maintain gusty NE winds with a continuation of\nrough, pounding surf, beach erosion around the times of high tide\nexacerbated by astronomical high tides. Additionally, deeper\nmoisture is forecast to surge northward and produce an excessive\nrain threat starting tonight along the Treasure coast and lift\nnorthward into Brevard county on Wed. Increased instability will\nsupport deeper convection (thunder) as well as higher rainfall \nrates in rainbands that cross the coast. Quick 1-3 inches will be\npossible across portions of the coast mainly south of the Cape\nwhich will produce a minor flood threat. \n\nThu-Tue...Pressure gradient relaxes a bit as low pressure\nretrogrades into the SE Gulf and weakens. Winds remain NE to E\naround high pressure to the north, breezy/gusty esp at the coast\nbut not as windy Thu/Fri. Reinforcing high pressure over the\neastern US builds southward so we should see an increase in the\neasterly wind flow this weekend with another round of Atlc\nrainbands pushing onshore depending on how much moisture will be\navailable. What is notable is the upper pattern starts to become\nmore El Nino-ish early next week lower latitude troughs traversing\nthe southern US. Too early to say with any confidence if there \nwill be a severe weather threat but will monitor trends.",
